\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Composite fields and a perturbative comparison}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{\citet{authors’ preprint, §5, pp. 22–23, (5.1)}{frohlich1981higgs}
use the squared scalar norm, field-strength composites for vector
bosons and fermionic composites in an #{SU(2)} model with
fundamental scalars and fermions.
Their neutral states are described using gauge-invariant fields.
The [scalar path pairing](fdip-011B) gives a finite gauge-invariant
observable with prescribed endpoint scalars.}

\p{We cite their polynomial correspondence for finite-dimensional
representations of a compact Lie gauge group (§5.1, Part I, p. 24
and §6, pp. 36–37). Fix a scalar orbit with trivial stabilizer and a
field in a specified irreducible representation. Evaluation at a reference
scalar identifies the components of that field with invariant
composites polynomial in the scalars and linear in the specified
field, modulo composites of this form vanishing on the chosen
orbit. With nontrivial stabilizer the authors use residual
representations and invariant strings (pp. 24–25).
The two inverse rotations in \ref{fdip-011G} exhibit the loss of
information in scalar path pairings on a finite graph.}

\p{Their comparison of correlations (§7.3, pp. 43–45,
(7.2)–(7.3)) concerns neutral fields in a gauge-fixed
perturbative expansion about a chosen scalar value. They normalize
each composite at that scalar to its elementary-field component.
The leading term is then the elementary-field correlation in that gauge. Further
terms contain scalar fluctuations. The authors expect an
asymptotic expansion and discuss suppression of these terms in
gauges where the Higgs radius is large compared with the mean
perturbative fluctuations. Renormalization enters this condition.
This cited dynamical comparison supplies no error bound for
the finite graph. An identification of poles, spectra or masses
would require further dynamical information.}
